<title>RUSSIA: AGROKHLEB-Russia wheat buys to rise, exports slip.</title>
<headline>AGROKHLEB-Russia wheat buys to rise, exports slip.</headline>
<text>
<p>The following is a direct contribution from SovEcon Ltd, an independent research organisation specialising in agricultural production and trade in the former Soviet Union.</p>
<p>MOSCOW, March 11 - Assuming a normal rate of wheat consumption, Russia's wheat consuming provinces, particularly in the European part of the country, are likely to increase wheat imports this spring.</p>
<p>Over the same time, it is expected that Russian wheat exports, particularly of milling wheat, will virtually dry up.</p>
<p>Continued Russian wheat exports in 1996/97 reflected not only better output in 1996 but also reduced demand for wheat, particularly feed wheat, due to a continued decline in livestock inventories.</p>
<p>Feed wheat deliveries, especially to Poland, have accounted for the bulk of the increase in wheat exports.</p>
<p>However, tight supplies have limited further exports in 1996/97. From November through January, Russia's wheat exports declined to 20,200 tonnes per month from 91,400 tonnes.</p>
